ZLIB DATA COMPRESSION LIBRARY

zlib 1.3.2.1 is a general purpose data compression library.  All the code is
thread safe (though see the FAQ for caveats).  The data format used by the zlib
library is described by RFCs (Request for Comments) 1950 to 1952 at
https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc1950 (zlib format), rfc1951 (deflate
format) and rfc1952 (gzip format).

All functions of the compression library are documented in the file zlib.h
(volunteer to write man pages welcome, contact zlib@gzip.org).  A usage example
of the library is given in the file test/example.c which also tests that
the library is working correctly.  Another example is given in the file
test/minigzip.c.  The reference C compression library itself is composed of all
source files in the root directory; a memory-safe Rust reimplementation of the
same library, exporting the same C ABI, is in the Cargo workspace under crates/
and is described below.

To compile all files and run the test program, follow the instructions given at
the top of Makefile.in.  In short "./configure; make test", and if that goes
well, "make install" should work for most flavors of Unix.  For Windows, use
one of the special makefiles in win32/ or contrib/vstudio/ .  For VMS, use
make_vms.com.

The compression and decompression engine has also been reimplemented in Rust,
in the Cargo workspace under crates/ .  It is a reimplementation and not a
binding: crates/zlib-rs holds every algorithm under #![forbid(unsafe_code)]
with an empty dependency list, and crates/libz-rs-sys holds the raw pointers a
C ABI unavoidably has, exposing that ABI so that existing callers link against
it unchanged.  zlib.h, zconf.h and zlib.map are unmodified; the C sources in
this directory are retained and are the reference the Rust code is diffed
against, so "./configure; make test" continues to build and test the C library
exactly as before, on machines with no Rust toolchain at all.

Each crate has a distinct job.  crates/zlib-rs is the algorithmic core --
deflate, inflate, the Huffman coder, both checksums, the gzip file layer and
the one-shot wrappers.  With no features it is no_std plus alloc and the gzip
file layer is not compiled at all, since a gzFile is a real file and only the
standard library can open one.  crates/libz-rs-sys is the C ABI layer and the
only shipped crate that uses unsafe, under #![deny(unsafe_op_in_unsafe_fn)] so
that each unsafe operation is scoped on its own and carries a comment naming
the invariant behind it; it builds as a cdylib, a staticlib and an rlib under
the library name z, which is why cargo emits libz.so and libz.a rather than
Rust-flavoured names.  crates/zlib-rs-differential is a development-only
harness that compiles the C sources in this directory as a test oracle;
neither shipped crate depends on it, so an ordinary build of the Rust library
never compiles them.

Two things follow about where unsafe lives, and both are worth stating exactly
rather than in round numbers.  Outside crates/libz-rs-sys, two dev-only files
declare or wrap raw entry points, and neither ships anywhere:
crates/zlib-rs-differential/src/oracle.rs, which is how the C oracle is called,
and crates/zlib-rs-differential/src/port.rs, whose boundary gates are how
everything else drives the C ABI.  Those gates are deliberately not part of the
shipped facade: AAP 0.6.1 fixes that facade's unsafe at exactly the six
unavoidable C-ABI site categories, and a test harness is not one of them.
Everything that uses the gates -- the three differential suites, the three
benchmark suites and all five fuzz targets -- contains no unsafe at all, and
neither does the safe core or any of its own test suites.  The only unsafe
outside the facade's src/ tree is a single slice cast in
crates/libz-rs-sys/tests/write_only_window.rs, which asserts a property of the
boundary from the crate that owns the boundary.

For a program that dynamically links libz, the exported symbol set is the same,
the z_stream and gz_header layouts are the same, the SONAME is still libz.so.1,
and zlib.h and zconf.h are installed unchanged, so that program keeps working
without being recompiled.  That is a measured result on one target,
x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu: it is where the staged library, the symbol diff and
the relinked C drivers are checked.  Staging the drop-in needs a linker that
accepts -soname and --version-script, so it is offered on the ELF platforms
that get zlib.map for the C shared library, and the CMake path stops with a
message on anything else -- not-UNIX, Apple, AIX and SunOS.  Building and
testing the Rust workspace is a wider claim than that and is made separately:
see the tier 1 note under "Notes for some targets" below.

Static linking is the one case that is not yet equivalent.  A Rust libz.a
carries the Rust runtime's own companion libraries, the set differs per target,
and the installed zlib.pc advertises none of it, so a consumer that static
links through that descriptor alone can fail to link.  zlib.pc.in and
zlib.pc.cmakein state the measurement and the remedy; treat it as an open
packaging gap rather than as settled.

The compressed bytes are the same as well, over the configurations the
differential tests compare: the encoder reproduces the reference
implementation's own heuristics rather than improving on them, and streams
interoperate in both directions -- what the C library deflates the Rust library
inflates, and the other way round.

To build and check the Rust library:

    make rust          # stage the drop-in libz under target/dropin/
    make rust-test     # link the unmodified C drivers against it and run them
    make rust-symbols  # diff the staged symbol set against a built C library

"make rust-symbols" is a comparison and not a listing.  It reports how many
symbols each library exports and which names one has that the other does not,
so it needs a C shared library built in this tree -- "make libz.so.1.3.2.1" or
ZLIBREF=/path/to/one -- and stops with that instruction when there is none.
"make rust" performs the same diff as one of its own checks, but only when such
a reference happens to be there already.

"make rust" and "make rust-test" do not need ./configure at all: they compose
the shared-object link from Makefile.in's own defaults and name the staged
library from ZLIB_VERSION in zlib.h, so they work on an unconfigured tree.
What ./configure adds is the C library build that "make rust-symbols" diffs
against, and a $(CARGO) recorded for you (unconfigured, name it yourself with
"make rust CARGO=cargo").

Four more targets complete the set:

    make rust-pc       # emit the pkg-config descriptor and gate a consumer
    make rust-header   # regenerate the C header with cbindgen and gate it
    make rust-msrv     # check the two shipped crates on the 1.80 floor
    make rust-clean    # remove what the Rust targets produced

"make rust-pc" is where the one packaging difference between the two
implementations is handled.  Dynamic linking is identical -- -lz resolves to
either library and a shared object records its own dependencies -- but a Rust
static archive carries the Rust runtime and so needs that runtime's native
libraries on a static link line, while the C libz.a needs none.  One zlib.pc
cannot say both, so that target writes a second descriptor beside the staged
library, with a Libs.private line derived for the target being built by
"rustc --print native-static-libs", and then compiles, links and runs a C
program from nothing but the flags pkg-config answers for it.  Install that
file in place of zlib.pc whenever you install the staged Rust library; the
target prints the copy command.  "make rust-test" runs it for you.  The CMake
build generates and installs the equivalent descriptor itself.

"make rust-header" wants cbindgen as well as a cargo reachable on PATH, and
"make rust-clean" wants no cargo at all.  The comment at the top of Makefile.in
describes all seven targets, including what each one does and does not
establish.

CMake can point the exported ZLIB::ZLIB and ZLIB::ZLIBSTATIC targets at the
Rust library instead of the C one:

    cmake -S . -B build -DZLIB_BUILD_RUST=ON

That option is off by default, and while it is off no Rust tool is probed and
the CMake build is exactly what it always was.  With it on, CMake performs the
same relink "make rust" does -- cargo's libz.a through zlib.map, with the same
SONAME and the same versioned symlink chain -- and then INSTALLS that set, so
"cmake --install" hands a consumer the complete drop-in and find_package(ZLIB)
resolves to it.  It is supported for a native ELF build: a shared library needs
a linker that takes -soname and --version-script, and a multi-config generator
or a cross-compilation toolchain is refused at configure time with the reason.

Inside the Rust workspace itself, cargo drives the build directly:

    cargo build --release
    cargo build --release -p libz-rs-sys --features libz-compat

The first builds the two shipped crates with their default features, which
already include the C ABI layer.  The second names that layer explicitly, which
is the form to use when the features are being chosen by hand, and it names the
package too: this is a virtual workspace, so a bare "--features libz-compat"
leaves it to cargo to decide which selected package the feature was meant for.
Saying "-p libz-rs-sys" says it instead.

Note that "cargo build" alone does NOT produce an installable shared library,
and the shortfall is measurable: the shared object cargo emits publishes 96
dynamic global symbols where the C library publishes 111.  It carries no
zlib.map version script (rustc attaches one of its own, and a second cannot be
added), it lacks gzprintf and gzvprintf (both variadic, so they come from the
small C shim in crates/libz-rs-sys/csrc/), and it exports internals that
zlib.map hides.  The static libz.a cargo builds IS complete -- the shim objects
are archived into it -- and "make rust" is what relinks the shared library from
that archive under zlib.map, creates the versioned symlink chain the SONAME
libz.so.1 requires, and checks the result against zlib.h and zlib.map: every
function zlib.h declares is exported and nothing else is, the version-node count
matches zlib.map, none of zlib.map's local names is exported, and the SONAME is
the derived one.

The further comparison against the C library's own symbol table is CONDITIONAL.
"make rust" performs it only when that library exists -- ZLIBREF defaults to the
C shared library built in this tree -- and prints a note saying it was skipped
otherwise, so a green "make rust" on a tree where the C library was never built
is not a statement about parity with C.  Two commands make that comparison
mandatory instead of optional, and both fail rather than skip:

    make                                    then  make rust
    make rust-symbols ZLIBREF=/path/to/libz.so.<version>

The symbols job of .github/workflows/rust.yml takes the first route on every
push, so a parity regression fails there whether or not it was checked by
hand.

Link against what "make rust" stages, not against cargo's target/<profile>
directory.  The versioned names are deliberately absent from the latter, so a
program linked against it builds and then binds the SYSTEM libz at run time;
cargo's build script leaves a README-cargo-artifacts.txt there saying so.  Use
ldd to see which library was bound rather than inferring it from a test that
passed -- that check is exactly what "make rust-test" performs.

Installing the Rust library means installing the same names the C build
installs, with the same SONAME and the same symlink topology: the real file
libz.so.1.3.2.1-motley, with libz.so.1 and libz.so as symlinks to it, and the
static libz.a.  "make rust" stages exactly that set under target/dropin/ and
names it from ZLIB_VERSION in zlib.h, ready to be copied into place.  Read the
note above about static linking before installing the libz.a for consumers to
link through zlib.pc.

Building the Rust library needs Rust 1.80 or later and cargo.  Optional
vectorised CRC-32 and Adler-32 backends are available behind the "simd" Cargo
feature; they change speed only, never the compressed bytes, and the paragraph
after the feature table below says how that is checked and where.

The workspace is edition 2021, and it has two floors rather than one, because
one of its three members never ships.  1.80 is the floor of the two crates that
do -- zlib-rs and libz-rs-sys, which both inherit rust-version from
[workspace.package] -- and it is the only floor that matters for building,
installing or using the library: "cargo build --release", "make rust" and
cmake -DZLIB_BUILD_RUST=ON each build just that pair.  The third member,
crates/zlib-rs-differential, is a dev-only harness that holds the C oracle and
the benchmarks, and it declares 1.86 of its own because that is the floor of
criterion, the benchmarking crate it depends on.  So the commands that reach it
-- "cargo test --workspace" and "cargo bench" -- want 1.86, and a 1.80 compiler
refuses them by name rather than failing part way through.  rust-toolchain.toml
pins the stable channel, comfortably above both, so cargo selects a suitable
compiler by itself; "make rust-msrv" is what checks the shipped pair on 1.80.

Selected Cargo features, with the crate that declares each one and its default.
This is not the whole surface -- "cargo metadata" is authoritative, and
rust/README.md carries the complete table with what each feature expands to:

    libz-compat   libz-rs-sys, on   the exported extern "C" surface
    gz            libz-rs-sys, on   the gzFile layer; it also turns on
                                    zlib-rs/std and the optional libc dependency
    libc          libz-rs-sys, off  names that optional dependency explicitly,
                                    so a downstream feature request keeps
                                    resolving; gz already enables it
    rust-api      zlib-rs, off      the idiomatic Rust surface
    simd          zlib-rs, off      autovectorised CRC-32 and Adler-32.  libz-rs-sys
                                    and zlib-rs-differential each declare a
                                    forwarding "simd" of their own, so a
                                    package-scoped request reaches the core
                                    wherever it is spelled
    std           zlib-rs, off      the core's file I/O; otherwise no_std.  A
                                    default libz-rs-sys build turns it on through
                                    gz

The "simd" feature deserves a plain description, because its name promises more
than it delivers.  Both backends behind it are ordinary portable integer code,
written so that LLVM has a shape it can autovectorise at opt-level 3; there is
not one architecture intrinsic in either, which is what lets the safe core keep
#![forbid(unsafe_code)].  Whether any vector instruction actually comes out is
therefore a property of the compiler and the target, not a promise made here.
Neither backend is chosen by asking the CPU what it supports either: the CRC-32
one performs no run-time feature test at all, and the Adler-32 one consults an
is_supported() that is a throughput hint rather than a correctness gate.  A
simd-enabled binary computes the right answer on a machine with no vector unit.
What the feature does promise is the answer: both compute the same checksums as
the scalar backends, on every input, so they change speed only.

Setting ZLIB_RS_SIMD=1 in the environment is how the simd feature is asked for
through make or CMake, both of which translate it into "simd" for cargo; a
direct cargo build takes "--features simd" instead, and the build script then
checks that the two requests agree rather than letting a mismatch pass
unnoticed.  ZLIB_RS_SIMD=0, or leaving it unset, builds the scalar backends.
The feature reaches the two checksums and nothing else, which is what keeps it
output neutral -- a checksum is one scalar however it is computed, and neither
module contains an architecture intrinsic: both are portable integer code that
LLVM autovectorises, and the only run-time probe anywhere is Adler-32's SSE2
check on x86 and x86_64, which selects between two arrangements of the same
arithmetic.

Neutrality is checked rather than argued, and here is exactly what checks it
and on which machines.  Three things run with the feature enabled: the crate's
own equivalence sweep, which compares the vectorised backend against the
scalar one at every path boundary and re-checks the dispatcher wiring; the
differential suites, which compare the port against the in-tree C
implementation over the committed corpus; and a 300-second cargo-fuzz run of
the checksum target that compares the two backends on fuzzer-chosen inputs.
The first two run in the all-features test pass, on all three platforms the
Rust build is gated on -- Linux, macOS and Windows.  The fuzz run is Linux
x86_64, as are the Miri and AddressSanitizer jobs.  A target outside that set
gets the same portable code and the same tests, but no measurement from us.

The gates themselves live in .github/workflows/rust.yml, and each one names
what it establishes: the differential suites against the C sources in
crates/zlib-rs-differential, Miri over the safe core with every feature enabled
(sharded by module and by test binary, since interpreting the whole crate in
one job does not finish), AddressSanitizer over the C ABI layer, the three
unmodified C drivers and the oracle harness, five cargo-fuzz targets at 300
seconds each plus the checksum target a second time with simd, the cbindgen
header diffed against zlib.h, the exported symbols and version nodes diffed
against the C library's own, and the criterion suites in benches/ measured
against the C oracle in the same process.

Questions about zlib should be sent to <zlib@gzip.org>, or to Gilles Vollant
<info@winimage.com> for the Windows DLL version.  The zlib home page is
https://zlib.net/ .  Before reporting a problem, please check this site to
verify that you have the latest version of zlib; otherwise get the latest
version and check whether the problem still exists or not.

PLEASE read the zlib FAQ https://zlib.net/zlib_faq.html before asking for help.

Mark Nelson <markn@ieee.org> wrote an article about zlib for the Jan.  1997
issue of Dr.  Dobb's Journal; a copy of the article is available at
https://zlib.net/nelson/ .

The changes made in version 1.3.2.1 are documented in the file ChangeLog.

Unsupported third party contributions are provided in directory contrib/ .

zlib is available in Java using the java.util.zip package. Follow the API
Documentation link at: https://docs.oracle.com/search/?q=java.util.zip .

A Perl interface to zlib and bzip2 written by Paul Marquess <pmqs@cpan.org>
can be found at https://github.com/pmqs/IO-Compress .

A Python interface to zlib written by A.M. Kuchling <amk@amk.ca> is
available in Python 1.5 and later versions, see
https://docs.python.org/3/library/zlib.html .

zlib is built into tcl: https://wiki.tcl-lang.org/page/zlib .

An experimental package to read and write files in .zip format, written on top
of zlib by Gilles Vollant <info@winimage.com>, is available in the
contrib/minizip directory of zlib.


Notes for some targets:

- For Windows DLL versions, please see win32/DLL_FAQ.txt

- For 64-bit Irix, deflate.c must be compiled without any optimization. With
  -O, one libpng test fails. The test works in 32 bit mode (with the -n32
  compiler flag). The compiler bug has been reported to SGI.

- zlib doesn't work with gcc 2.6.3 on a DEC 3000/300LX under OSF/1 2.1 it works
  when compiled with cc.

- On Digital Unix 4.0D (formerly OSF/1) on AlphaServer, the cc option -std1 is
  necessary to get gzprintf working correctly. This is done by configure.

- zlib doesn't work on HP-UX 9.05 with some versions of /bin/cc. It works with
  other compilers. Use "make test" to check your compiler.

- For PalmOs, see https://palmzlib.sourceforge.net/

- The Rust workspace builds and its test suite runs on Rust's tier 1 targets,
  which is what the Rust CI matrix covers on Linux, macOS and Windows. The
  installable drop-in library is a narrower claim than that: staging it needs a
  linker that accepts -soname and --version-script, so it is offered on ELF
  platforms only -- Windows, Apple, AIX and SunOS are refused with a message --
  and it is verified on x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu. The platform makefiles in
  this distribution -- amiga, msdos, os400, qnx, watcom and win32 -- build the
  C sources and are unaffected.


